1
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:08,050
A founder records every meeting.

2
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:11,030
After a few months, there are hundreds of hours of transcripts,

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,930
and nobody can find the decision they need when it matters.

4
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:16,750
I built Meeting Memory to fix that.

5
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:20,350
It turns meeting transcripts into memory you can question,

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,270
and it proves every answer with the speaker's exact words.

7
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:31,850
Each meeting is read once, in order, and turned into a ledger: decisions, proposals,

8
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:33,810
actions, figures and objections.

9
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:37,570
Every entry points back to the exact line it came from.

10
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:40,370
The transcripts stay the source of truth,

11
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:43,650
stored in SQLite with a keyword index and vector search.

12
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:48,510
When a question comes in, the agent works on one project only.

13
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:52,550
It searches that project's ledger and transcripts, writes an answer,

14
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,310
and code checks it before you see it.

15
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:59,190
Because each question touches one project, the cost stays flat,

16
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:00,670
whether there are a hundred meetings or ten thousand.

17
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:07,090
The hard part is memory over time.

18
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:10,819
A decision in the third meeting can replace one from the first.

19
00:01:10,669 --> 00:01:12,479
That isn't left to the model.

20
00:01:12,329 --> 00:01:14,579
Code orders every decision by meeting,

21
00:01:14,429 --> 00:01:17,839
and marks the latest one on each topic as current.

22
00:01:17,689 --> 00:01:21,979
This team planned a titanium back, argued about it across two meetings,

23
00:01:21,829 --> 00:01:24,659
and finally dropped it to meet the cost target.

24
00:01:24,509 --> 00:01:26,879
The earlier decisions are struck through as replaced,

25
00:01:26,729 --> 00:01:29,719
and the final one is marked current.

26
00:01:29,569 --> 00:01:32,219
And when someone refers back to "the other one",

27
00:01:32,069 --> 00:01:34,239
the extractor links it to the earlier option,

28
00:01:34,089 --> 00:01:39,899
because it reads each meeting with the ledger so far.

29
00:01:39,749 --> 00:01:41,819
Let's ask a question.

30
00:01:41,669 --> 00:01:46,339
While it runs, the page shows the agent's real steps: find the project,

31
00:01:46,229 --> 00:01:50,499
search its meetings, read and write, then check every quote.

32
00:01:50,349 --> 00:01:54,559
The answer comes back in a sentence or two, with up to three quotes.

33
00:01:54,409 --> 00:01:57,119
Each quote has been checked word for word against the transcript,

34
00:01:57,509 --> 00:02:00,059
so it can't be paraphrased or made up.

35
00:01:59,909 --> 00:02:04,119
If you want more proof, open it in the transcript, read the whole meeting,

36
00:02:03,969 --> 00:02:05,499
or download the original file.

37
00:02:08,489 --> 00:02:11,399
Saying no matters as much as answering.

38
00:02:11,249 --> 00:02:15,469
Ask what the designers were paid, and it tells you the meetings never discussed it,

39
00:02:15,319 --> 00:02:16,469
instead of guessing.

40
00:02:16,319 --> 00:02:21,389
Ask why a team chose an LCD screen when they actually rejected it,

41
00:02:21,239 --> 00:02:22,829
and it corrects the question.

42
00:02:22,679 --> 00:02:27,649
Ask without naming a project, and it lists the projects that discuss it most,

43
00:02:27,499 --> 00:02:30,169
with the line that matched, and lets you choose.

44
00:02:33,139 --> 00:02:38,709
Before a team's final meeting, it writes a brief: what was decided, what changed,

45
00:02:38,559 --> 00:02:42,429
open actions and who owns them, and disagreements still open.

46
00:02:42,599 --> 00:02:45,409
It only reads the meetings that have already happened.

47
00:02:45,559 --> 00:02:49,789
That cutoff is enforced in code, so the final meeting can't leak in.

48
00:02:49,699 --> 00:02:51,189
Every point cites its source.

49
00:02:54,039 --> 00:02:57,469
Where the model stops and code starts was a deliberate choice.

50
00:02:57,399 --> 00:03:03,409
Anything with one right answer is plain code: parsing, ordering, what is current,

51
00:03:03,259 --> 00:03:06,009
quote checks, and the output format.

52
00:03:05,859 --> 00:03:10,549
The model only does what rules can't: reading messy speech,

53
00:03:10,399 --> 00:03:12,089
and writing clear answers.

54
00:03:11,939 --> 00:03:15,029
Code even audits the model's own notes.

55
00:03:14,879 --> 00:03:18,298
It found sixteen figures that nobody actually said,

56
00:03:18,148 --> 00:03:21,598
and one price stored in Euros that was spoken in Francs.

57
00:03:21,448 --> 00:03:27,798
Those are flagged, and never repeated as fact.

58
00:03:27,648 --> 00:03:29,178
Does it work?

59
00:03:29,028 --> 00:03:33,398
On ten questions of my own, checked by hand against the transcripts,

60
00:03:33,248 --> 00:03:34,178
it got eight right.

61
00:03:34,028 --> 00:03:38,038
On seven questions that should get a no, it said no all seven times.

62
00:03:38,708 --> 00:03:43,098
All twenty-five graded answers pass the word-for-word quote check.

63
00:03:43,248 --> 00:03:46,358
Three answers it got wrong are written up, with their causes.

64
00:03:47,268 --> 00:03:51,918
Ingesting a thousand meetings costs about one dollar sixty with Claude Haiku on the Batch API,

65
00:03:51,768 --> 00:03:53,818
and takes under four hours.

66
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:58,618
The free tiers I built on broke at this size: fifty requests a day,

67
00:03:58,468 --> 00:04:00,998
and an eight-thousand-token cap per request.

68
00:04:03,948 --> 00:04:07,818
Every design decision is logged, with what I rejected and why.

69
00:04:07,988 --> 00:04:11,558
For production, it would need paid models, Postgres,

70
00:04:11,408 --> 00:04:13,698
and a login with review for uploaded meetings.

71
00:04:14,528 --> 00:04:17,678
The code, the write-up and the answers are all in the repository.

72
00:04:18,508 --> 00:04:19,658
Thank you.
